{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011zW3vwesTRNbYPQm2F3kKP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\n2\t### class Engine\n3\t\n4\t- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n5\t- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n6\t- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listeners, no AudioContext (`audio.counts` still counts), no panels; errors go to `engin…\n7\t- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throw…\n8\t- `readonly input: Input`\n9\t- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n10\t- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n11\t- `readonly context: GameContext`\n12\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n13\t- `readonly particles: Particles`\n14\t- `readonly hud: Hud`\n15\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n16\t- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n17\t- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n18\t- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n19\t- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\n20\t- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n21\t- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n22\t- `frame: number`\n23\t- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n24\t- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n25\t- `time: number`\n26\t- `readonly renderer: View`\n27\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n28\t- `camera: CameraRig`\n29\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n30\t- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n31\t- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n32\t- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n33\t- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n34\t- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\n35\t- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\n36\t- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n37\t- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n38\t- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\n39\t- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n40\t- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n41\t- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n42\t- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n43\t- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n44\t- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n45\t- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\n46\t- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n47\t- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n48\t- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n49\t- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n50\t- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n51\t- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n52\t- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n53\t- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n54\t- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n55\t- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\n56\t- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n57\t- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n58\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\n59\t- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n60\t- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n61\t- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n62\t- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manua…\n63\t- `snapshot(): Promise<Snapshot>`: The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the game's own state (`Game.snapshot`). Plain JSON. Taken once the las…\n64\t- `restore(saved: Snapshot): Promise<void>`: Put the running level back to a snapshot of it: the same game (by name), already loaded; nothing in between is replayed. The level's recording starts again from it (`Recording.from`), so what is played next replays with `replay()`. Live input held now is let…\n65\t- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the simulation: game time, every physics body (exact), `ctx.random`'s state and the game's own (`Game.fingerprint`). Two runs that should be the same (a replay) compare equal. Not the `status()` line: a debug line may carry measurements (a step's mi…\n66\t\n67\t\n68\t### interface EngineOptions\n69\t\n70\t- `container?: HTMLElement`\n71\t- `resolution?: Resolution`\n72\t- `aspect?: AspectMode`: `adaptive` (default): the art height stays `resolution.height` and the art width follows the screen's aspect (portrait phones fill the screen). `fixed`: always exactly `resolution`, letterboxed. `fill`: adjusts both art dimensions to cover the viewport at an…\n73\t- `maxFps?: number`: Cap the render loop (simulation keeps its fixed 60 Hz physics step). Default 60, so 120 Hz phones don't run the whole pipeline twice per game frame. 0 = display rate.\n74\t- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow map quality, or `auto` (default: low on touch devices, else medium; lowered once on a slow start).\n75\t- `loadingScreen?: boolean`: Pixel-styled loading indicator while the renderer, physics and assets load. Default true.\n76\t- `mode?: RenderMode`\n77\t- `edges?: EdgeSettings`\n78\t- `camera?: CameraConfig`: Camera preset + parameters. Pick one per game; not meant to change during play.\n79\t- `filters?: readonly string[]`: Post filters (ids from FILTERS) applied in Pixel mode, in order.\n80\t- `look?: Look`: A full look: pixel art or clean rendering and filters per layer (characters & objects, environment) plus whole-scene filters (render/look.ts). Wins over `filters` and `edges`.\n81\t- `seed?: number`: Seed for `ctx.random`: each level gets its own stream from this and the game's name, so the same seed replays the same game. Default 1. `?seed=` in the URL.\n82\t- `record?: number`: Frames of input the engine keeps for each level (`recording()`). Default `MAX_FRAMES`, an hour at 60 fps (up to some 20 MB of JSON with the mouse moving all the time); 0 records nothing.\n83\t- `debugUI?: boolean`: Debug panel. Default: on in dev (`vite`) or with ?debug=1, off in production builds.\n84\t- `debugKeys?: DebugKeysOption`: Engine hotkeys (P Pixel/Raw, R resolution, ` debug panel, [ ] looks, M mute): developer tools. Default: on with the debug panel (dev, ?debug=1), off in a shipped game, whose players have the same options in the settings. `true` / `false`: always on / off; an…\n85\t- `settings?: { button?: TouchCorner | boolean; key?: string | false }`: The player's settings (`engine.settings`): look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate, sound and debug info, in a panel the ⚙ button opens. `button`: its corner (default, or true: 'top-right'; on a mouse it shows while the pointer moves), or false for n…\n86\t- `touch?: boolean`: On-screen joystick + buttons. Default: auto (coarse pointer), or ?touch=1 / ?touch=0.\n87\t- `touchButtons?: readonly TouchButton[]`: On-screen action buttons. Default: the platformer's (DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS).\n88\t- `touchBar?: boolean`: The touch top bar of the engine hotkeys (ⓘ debug panel, P, R, ◐ looks, ♪ mute), on a touch screen while they are on (see `debugKeys`). Default true; false hides it even then, unless the debug panel is on (`?debug=1`, dev builds).\n89\t- `background?: number`\n90\t\n91\t\n92\t### interface Game\n93\t\n94\tA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\n95\tlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n96\t\n97\t- `readonly name: string`\n98\t- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (`npm run replay`, src/games.…\n99\t- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n100\t- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n101\t- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n102\t- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is…\n103\t- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n104\t- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n105\t- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n106\t- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n107\t- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay mu…\n108\t- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\n109\t- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n110\t- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `re…\n111\t- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in first person).\n112\t- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, timers, listeners, HMR hooks.\n113\t\n114\t\n115\t### interface GameContext\n116\t\n117\t- `readonly engine: Engine`\n118\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n119\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n120\t- `readonly input: Input`\n121\t- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n122\t- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n123\t- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n124\t- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n125\t- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n126\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n127\t- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n128\t- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n129\t- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n130\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n131\t\n132\t\n133\t### interface HeadlessOptions\n134\t\n135\t`Engine.headless`: the page's options (those about the page do nothing), and where it runs.\n136\t\n137\t- `viewport?: Viewport`: The viewport the art resolution and framing come from, as a page's would (default 960×540 at DPR 1). Pointer aim and HUD layout depend on it: a replay uses its recording's (`Recording.view`).\n138\t- `assets?: AssetReader`: How models and packs are read: `nodeAssets()` (src/engine/node.ts) reads `public/`.\n139\t\n140\t\n141\t### class Settings\n142\t\n143\t`engine.settings`: the player's settings, applied to the engine and saved. A field the page URL\n144\tsets (`?look=`, `?mode=`, `?res=`, `?quality=`, `?fps=`, `?debug=`: review flags) wins over a\n145\tsaved one at start (which stays saved). `open`, `close` and `toggle` show and hide the\n146\tsettings panel.\n147\t\n148\t- `new Settings(host: SettingsHost, key: string | null)`\n149\t- `readonly key: string | null`\n150\t- `get values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>`: The player's choices in effect: fields left out are the game's (or a review flag's).\n151\t- `get isOpen: boolean`\n152\t- `set(patch: PlayerSettings): void`: Change some settings: applied at once and saved. A field set to `undefined` goes back to the game's.\n153\t- `reset(): void`: Forget every choice, the held ones too: the game's own picture again.\n154\t- `replace(values: PlayerSettings): void`: Make `values` the settings, changing only the fields that differ: the ones it leaves out go back to the game's.\n155\t- `onChange(fn: (values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>) => void): () => void`: Called with the values after every change; returns the unsubscribe.\n156\t- `open(): void`\n157\t- `close(): void`\n158\t- `toggle(): void`\n159\t\n160\t\n161\t### class CameraRig\n162\t\n163\tBase rig: owns the three.js camera, zoom and the ground-plane movement basis.\n164\t\n165\t- `new CameraRig(config: CameraConfig)`\n166\t- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n167\t- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera | PerspectiveCamera`\n168\t- `readonly focus: Vector3`: World point the rig is centred on (used for shadow framing).\n169\t- `zoom: number`\n170\t- `readonly minZoom: number`\n171\t- `readonly maxZoom: number`\n172\t- `readonly zoomable: boolean`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n173\t- `readonly hidesTarget: boolean`: First person hides the player model.\n174\t- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n175\t- `controlsCharacter: boolean`: False while the free camera is flying (input drives the camera, not the player).\n176\t- `snap: boolean`: Pixel-snap the camera (ortho only).\n177\t- `aspect: number`: Viewport aspect (width / height), following the art resolution (see `setAspect`).\n178\t- `rayAt(x: number, y: number, origin: Vector3, dir: Vector3): void`: The ray through screen point (x, y) in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\n179\t- `setZoom(zoom: number): void`\n180\t- `setAspect(aspect: number): void`: Match the art resolution's aspect (adaptive framing changes it with the screen). `update()` calls this from `CameraUpdate.resolution`; the engine also calls it on swap.\n181\t- `teleport(target: Vector3): void`: Place the camera without smoothing.\n182\t- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3 }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n183\t- `update(u: CameraUpdate): void`\n184\t- `describe(): Record<string, unknown>`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n185\t- `state(): CameraRigState`: The rig as data, exact (`engine.snapshot()`).\n186\t- `setState(s: CameraRigState): void`: Put the rig back where `state()` found it (the same preset).\n187\t\n188\t\n189\t### class ThirdPersonRig\n190\t\n191\tOver-the-shoulder orbit camera: mouse-drag or Q/E to orbit, wheel to zoom, avoids walls.\n192\t\n193\t- `new ThirdPersonRig(config: CameraConfig = {})`\n194\t- `readonly preset: \"third\"`\n195\t- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera`\n196\t- `yaw: number`\n197\t- `pitch: number`\n198\t- `describe(): { yaw: number; pitch: number; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n199\t\n200\t\n201\t### interface LightRequestOptions\n202\t\n203\t- `position?: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]`: World position (copied). Ignored while `follow` is set (then it is `follow` + `offset`).\n204\t- `follow?: Object3D | null`: Follow an object's world position every frame (a projectile, a monster, the hero).\n205\t- `offset?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Offset from `follow` (world axes).\n206\t- `color?: ColorRepresentation`\n207\t- `intensity?: number`: Point light intensity at full strength (before flicker and fades). Default 4.\n208\t- `radius?: number`: Reach in metres (PointLight.distance: light is 0 beyond it). Default 6.\n209\t- `decay?: number`: Distance falloff exponent (PointLight.decay; a uniform, free to change). Default 1.2.\n210\t- `flicker?: FlickerPreset`\n211\t- `priority?: number`: Importance multiplier: the hero's light 4, a loot beam 2, a torch 1 (default).\n212\t- `fadeIn?: number`: Fade times in seconds (defaults 0.25 in, 0.2 out).\n213\t- `fadeOut?: number`\n214\t- `lifetime?: number`: Release automatically after this many seconds (spell flashes, explosions).\n215\t- `name?: string`: Optional label for debugging (`pool.describe()`).\n216\t\n217\t\n218\t### class CharacterController\n219\t\n220\tKinematic capsule driven by Rapier's KinematicCharacterController, with classic\n221\tplatformer affordances (coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height).\n222\t\n223\tThe body moves in continuous world space; the visual model is interpolated by\n224\tPhysics.bind and only *looks* pixel-aligned through the render pipeline.\n225\t\n226\t- `new CharacterController(physics: Physics, options: CharacterOptions)`\n227\t- `readonly body: RAPIER.RigidBody`\n228\t- `readonly collider: RAPIER.Collider`\n229\t- `readonly controller: RAPIER.KinematicCharacterController`\n230\t- `readonly velocity: Vector3`\n231\t- `grounded: boolean`\n232\t- `facing: number`\n233\t- `get feet: Vector3`: Feet position (bottom of the capsule), continuous world space.\n234\t- `interpolatedFeet(alpha: number): Vector3`: Feet position blended between the previous and current physics step (render use).\n235\t- `setInput(move: Vector3, jumpPressed: boolean, jumpHeld: boolean): void`: Set desired horizontal move direction (world space, length ≤ 1) and jump input.\n236\t- `teleport(position: [number, number, number]): void`\n237\t- `fixedUpdate(dt: number): void`: Call from Physics.update's fixedUpdate callback.\n238\t- `attachAnimations(model: Object3D, clips: readonly AnimationClip[]): void`: Attach a model's animation clips (expects Idle / Walk / Jump).\n239\t- `get animation: CharacterAnim`\n240\t- `updateVisual(model: Object3D, dt: number): void`: Per render frame: pick animation state and advance the mixer.\n241\t\n242\t\n243\t### class Input\n244\t\n245\tKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n246\t- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n247\t  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n248\t- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n249\t  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n250\t  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n251\t- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n252\t  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n253\t  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n254\t  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n255\t  a frame is not lost.\n256\t- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n257\t\n258\t- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n259\t- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n260\t- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n261\t- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n262\t- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n263\t- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n264\t- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n265\t- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n266\t- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n267\t- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n268\t- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n269\t- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n270\t- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n271\t- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n272\t- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n273\t- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n274\t- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n275\t- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n276\t- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n277\t- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n278\t- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n279\t- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n280\t- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n281\t- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n282\t- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n283\t- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n284\t- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n285\t- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n286\t- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n287\t- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n288\t- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n289\t- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n290\t- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n291\t- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n292\t- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n293\t- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n294\t- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n295\t- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n296\t- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n297\t- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n298\t- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n299\t- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n300\t- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n301\t- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n302\t\n303\t\n304\t\n305\t### class Hud\n306\t\n307\tPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\n308\tthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\n309\tlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n310\t(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n311\t\n312\tRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n313\t(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\n314\tresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n315\t\n316\t  ctx.hud.clear();\n317\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n318\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n319\t\n320\t- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n321\t- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n322\t- `visible: boolean`\n323\t- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n324\t- `get height: number`\n325\t- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n326\t- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n327\t- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n328\t- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n329\t- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n330\t- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n331\t- `dispose(): void`\n332\t\n333\t\n334\t\n335\t### class AudioManager\n336\t\n337\tWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\n338\tsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\n339\tpersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n340\t\n341\tBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\n342\tfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n343\t(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\n344\twas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n345\t\n346\t- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n347\t- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n348\t- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n349\t- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n350\t- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n351\t- `context: AudioContext | null`\n352\t- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n353\t- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n354\t- `get muted: boolean`\n355\t- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n356\t- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n357\t- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n358\t- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n359\t- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n360\t- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n361\t- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n362\t- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n363\t- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n364\t- `stopMusic(): void`\n365\t- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n366\t- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n367\t- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n368\t- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n369\t- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n370\t- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n371\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n372\t\n373\t\n374\t### interface SoundDef\n375\t\n376\t- `wave: Wave`\n377\t- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n378\t- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n379\t- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n380\t- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n381\t- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n382\t- `sustain?: number`\n383\t- `decay?: number`\n384\t- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n385\t- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n386\t- `arpRate?: number`\n387\t- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n388\t- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n389\t- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n390\t- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n391\t\n392\t\n393\t### interface Song\n394\t\n395\tA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\n396\twhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\n397\tEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\n398\tnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n399\t\n400\t- `bpm: number`\n401\t- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n402\t- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n403\t- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n404\t- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n405\t- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n406\t\n407\t\n408\t\n409\t### class Timeline\n410\t\n411\t- `new Timeline(o: TimelineOptions)`\n412\t- `readonly duration: number`\n413\t- `time: number`: The playhead (s).\n414\t- `playing: boolean`\n415\t- `speed: number`: Playback rate (1: real time, 0.5: slow motion).\n416\t- `ended: boolean`: True once the end has been reached (by playing or skipping).\n417\t- `readonly fired: string[]`: Cue names in the order they fired (tests and tools).\n418\t- `play(): void`\n419\t- `pause(): void`\n420\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Advance by `dt` seconds of game time (times `speed`) while playing, firing the cues passed.\n421\t- `seek(t: number): void`: Move the playhead to `t` without firing the cues in between (scrubbing). Cues before `t` count as done and cues after it will fire again (going back re-arms them). Ignored while skipping (a cue that loops back cannot undo a skip).\n422\t- `skip(): void`: Jump to the end: the cues not yet fired run (except the cosmetic ones), then `onEnd`. Cues after `duration` never fire, played or skipped.\n423\t- `camera(t = this.time): CameraShot | null`: Where the camera is at `t` (default now): on each key at its time, gliding between keys on a smooth curve (Catmull-Rom tangents, so the speed has no jolt at a key; the first and last key of each shot ease in and out). A `cut` key starts a new shot. Null witho…\n424\t- `line(t = this.time): Line | null`: The subtitle showing at `t` (default now), or null.\n425\t- `bars(t = this.time): number`: How far the letterbox bars are in (0..1) at `t` (default now): sliding in at the start, out at the end.\n426\t\n427\t\n428\t### interface TimelineOptions\n429\t\n430\t- `shots?: readonly CameraKey[]`\n431\t- `cues?: readonly Cue[]`\n432\t- `lines?: readonly Line[]`\n433\t- `duration?: number`: Length (s); default the last key, cue or line.\n434\t- `bars?: number`: Seconds the letterbox bars take to slide in and out (default 0.6; 0: no bars).\n435\t- `onEnd?: () => void`: Called once when the playhead reaches the end (or the scene is skipped).\n436\t\n437\t\n438\t### class NavGrid\n439\t\n440\t- `new NavGrid(width: number, height: number, o: NavGridOptions = {})`\n441\t- `static fromRows(rows: readonly string[], o: NavGridOptions & { blocked?: string } = {}): NavGrid`: From an ASCII map: `blocked` characters are closed, everything else open. Default '# ': walls and pits (a ' ' has no floor in RoomKit's maps); past the end of a short row is a wall.\n442\t- `readonly width: number`\n443\t- `readonly height: number`\n444\t- `readonly cell: number`\n445\t- `readonly ox: number`: World x, z of cell (0, 0)'s corner.\n446\t- `readonly oz: number`\n447\t- `readonly open: Uint8Array`: 1 = walkable.\n448\t- `version: number`: Bumped by `setOpen`: flow fields rebuild.\n449\t- `flowBuilds: number`: How many times a flow field was built (tools: should be one per target cell change).\n450\t- `col(x: number): number`\n451\t- `row(z: number): number`\n452\t- `centerX(col: number): number`: World x, z of a cell's centre.\n453\t- `centerZ(row: number): number`\n454\t- `isOpen(col: number, row: number): boolean`\n455\t- `walkable(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is the world point on a walkable cell?\n456\t- `setOpen(col: number, row: number, open: boolean): void`\n457\t- `path(from: readonly [number, number, number], to: readonly [number, number, number]): [number, number, number][] | null`: Shortest path between two world points (x, z used; y kept from `from`): A* over cells, no corner cutting, then smoothed so it only turns where a wall is in the way. Null when there is no way. The first point is `from`, the last `to` (snapped into its cell).\n458\t- `smooth(pts: [number, number, number][]): [number, number, number][]`: Drop every point the path can skip in a straight line.\n459\t- `lineOfSight(ax: number, az: number, bx: number, bz: number, radius = 0): boolean`: Can you see (or walk straight) from a to b? Walks the cells under the segment (a DDA); `radius` > 0 also tests two lines offset sideways (a body that wide fits).\n460\t- `castWall(ax: number, az: number, dx: number, dz: number, max: number): number`: Distance from a along a direction to the first closed cell (at most `max`): the same cell walk as `lineOfSight`, so a vision cone drawn with it shows exactly what a guard can see.\n461\t- `flowTo(x: number, z: number): boolean`: A flow field toward the world point (x, z): the walking distance from every cell to it. Rebuilt only when the target changes cell or the grid changed (`version`). Returns whether it was rebuilt.\n462\t- `flowDistance(x: number, z: number): number`: Walking distance to the flow target from a world point (Infinity: no way).\n463\t- `flowDirection(x: number, z: number, out: [number, number]): boolean`: Which way to go from (x, z) toward the flow target: the unit direction to the lowest-distance neighbour (into `out` [x, z]). Returns false at the target or with no way.\n464\t\n465\t\n466\t### class Ragdoll\n467\t\n468\t- `new Ragdoll(physics: Physics, root: Object3D, parts: readonly RagdollPart[], o: RagdollOptions = {})`\n469\t- `readonly parts: readonly RagdollPart[]`\n470\t- `readonly o: Required<RagdollOptions>`\n471\t- `readonly root: Object3D`\n472\t- `get active: boolean`: Whether the bodies exist (a `physics.clear()`, a level unload, removes them: then it is off).\n473\t- `get bodies: RAPIER.RigidBody[]`: The bodies (root part first), while active.\n474\t- `enable(o: { velocity?: Vec3; spin?: Vec3 } = {}): void`: Make the bodies from the model's current pose, moving at `velocity` (and spinning at `spin`, rad/s).\n475\t- `push(at: Vec3 | Vector3, velocity: Vec3): void`: Knock the part nearest `at`: it gains `velocity` (m/s, applied there as an impulse of its own mass, so the same push works whatever the ragdoll's density), and its joints pass some of it on.\n476\t- `mass(): number`: Total mass of the parts (world units), while active.\n477\t- `speed(): number`: Fastest part (m/s): settled when this stays low.\n478\t- `rootPose(out = new Vector3()): { at: Vector3; faceUp: boolean; heading: number }`: Where the root part (the pelvis) is and how it lies: face up (on its back: its forward, +Z, points up) or down, and the heading (yaw) a character lying like that has: on its back its head is behind it (−Z), on its front ahead (+Z). That is the heading to get…\n479\t- `sync(): void`: Write the bodies into the joints (between the last two physics steps). Call every frame after the physics update.\n480\t- `release(o: { ground?: number | ((x: number, z: number) => number); heading?: number; offset?: Vec3 } = {}): { apply(w: number): void }`: Remove the bodies and hand the pose back. The model root moves under the root part (feet at `ground`: a height, or a function of x, z called once the bodies are gone; turned to `heading`, default `rootPose().heading`) without moving the joints in the world; t…\n481\t- `disable(): void`: Remove the bodies (the joints keep the last pose).\n482\t\n483\t\n484\t### class WaterSurface\n485\t\n486\t- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n487\t- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n488\t- `waves: Wave[]`\n489\t- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n490\t- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n491\t- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n492\t- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n493\t- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n494\t- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n495\t- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n496\t- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n497\t\n498\t\n499\t### class Precipitation\n500\t\n501\t- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n502\t- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n503\t- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n504\t- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n505\t- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n506\t- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n507\t- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n508\t- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n509\t- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n510\t\n511\t\n512\t### groundFog()\n513\t\n514\t```ts\n515\tfunction groundFog(o: { color?: number; top?: number; amount?: number; near?: number; far?: number } = {}): { node: Node; color: UniformNode<\"color\", Color>; amount: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; top: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; }\n516\t```\n517\t\n518\tFog for `scene.fogNode` with live settings (uniforms: changing them never recompiles):\n519\tground mist that thickens below `top` (m) and a haze that grows with distance from the\n520\tcamera, both up to `amount` (0..1), in `color` (match the sky).\n521\t\n522\t  const mist = groundFog({ color: s.sky, top: 1.5 });\n523\t  (scene as { fogNode: unknown }).fogNode = mist.node;\n524\t  mist.amount.value = 0.8; mist.color.value.setHex(s.sky);\n525\t\n526\t\n527\t### applySky()\n528\t\n529\t```ts\n530\tfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n531\t```\n532\t\n533\tPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n534\t\n535\t\n536\t### skyAt()\n537\t\n538\t```ts\n539\tfunction skyAt(hour: number): SkyState\n540\t```\n541\t\n542\tThe sky at `hour` (wraps around 24).\n543\t\n544\t\n545\t### compileClips()\n546\t\n547\t```ts\n548\tfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n549\t```\n550\t\n551\t\n552\t### gaitClip()\n553\t\n554\t```ts\n555\tfunction gaitClip(rig: RigSpec, g: GaitSpec): ClipDef\n556\t```\n557\t\n558\t\n559\t### interface ClipDef\n560\t\n561\t- `name: string`\n562\t- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n563\t- `loop?: boolean`\n564\t- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n565\t- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n566\t- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instead of sinking.\n567\t- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n568\t- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n569\t- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n570\t- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n571\t- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n572\t- `notes?: string`\n573\t\n574\t\n575\t\n576\t### class SpringChain\n577\t\n578\tA chain of points hanging off a moving root. Call `update(dt, root, basis)` every frame\n579\twith the root's world position and its orientation (three columns: right, up, forward),\n580\tthen read `points` (root first).\n581\t\n582\t- `new SpringChain(o: SpringChainOptions)`\n583\t- `readonly points: Vec3[]`\n584\t- `o: Required<SpringChainOptions>`\n585\t- `reset(root: Vec3, basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): void`: Put the chain straight out in its rest pose (no swing).\n586\t- `update(dt: number, root: Vec3, basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): void`\n587\t- `swing(basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): number`: How far the tip is from where the rest pose puts it (m): how much it is swinging.\n588\t\n589\t\n590\t### class Decals\n591\t\n592\t- `new Decals(scene: Scene, o: { capacity?: number } = {})`\n593\t- `readonly meshes: Map<\"ring\" | \"crack\" | \"footprint\" | \"scorch\" | \"splat\", InstancedMesh<BufferGeometry<NormalBufferAttributes, BufferGeometryEventMap>, Material<...> | Material…`\n594\t- `readonly o: { capacity?: number }`\n595\t- `add(shape: DecalShape, at: Vector3Like, normal: Vector3Like, o: DecalOptions = {}): number`: Lay a mark at `at` on a surface facing `normal`. Returns its index in that shape's pool.\n596\t- `count(shape?: DecalShape): number`: Marks alive (per shape or all).\n597\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Per frame: age the marks; old ones dither away over a second.\n598\t- `clear(): void`: Remove every mark (the meshes stay for reuse).\n599\t- `dispose(): void`\n600\t\n601\t\n602\t### spatialize()\n603\t\n604\t```ts\n605\tfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n606\t```\n607\t\n608\tGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n609\t\n610\t\n611\t### renderSound()\n612\t\n613\t```ts\n614\tfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n615\t```\n616\t\n617\tRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n618\t\n619\t\n620\t### parseSong()\n621\t\n622\t```ts\n623\tfunction parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong\n624\t```\n625\t\n626\tFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n627\t\n628\t\n629\t\n630\t### class Particles\n631\t\n632\tPixel-art particles: CPU-simulated pools, drawn as camera-facing square sprites in flat\n633\tpalette colors. One emitter (one instanced draw call, one pooled instance buffer) per\n634\tpreset; sizes are whole art pixels, so in Pixel mode a particle is a crisp N×N block.\n635\tAvailable as `ctx.particles`; emitters are created on first use and freed by `clear()`\n636\t(the engine calls it when a level unloads).\n637\t\n638\t  ctx.particles.burst('dust', hero.feet);\n639\t  ctx.particles.burst('skid', feet, { direction: [-vx, 0.3, -vz], count: 2 });\n640\t\n641\t- `new Particles(view: () => ParticleView)`\n642\t- `readonly group: Group<Object3DEventMap>`: Engine-owned group holding every emitter.\n643\t- `readonly presets: Map<string, ParticlePreset>`: Preset registry: the built-ins plus anything registered.\n644\t- `readonly pixelWorld: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`: World units per art pixel at the camera focus (updated every frame).\n645\t- `register(name: string, preset: ParticlePreset): void`: Add or replace a named preset. Replacing frees the old preset's emitter.\n646\t- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is full, 0 for an unknown preset). Inline preset objects share one emitter per distinct content, so `burst({ ...literal }, at)` every…\n647\t- `get alive: number`: Particles alive right now, over all emitters.\n648\t- `get emitterCount: number`: Emitters (= draw calls at most) currently allocated.\n649\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Simulate and upload. Called by the engine every frame (frozen while paused).\n650\t- `reseed(rng: Rng): void`: Take bursts' randomness from `rng` (the engine passes each level's `ctx.random` fork).\n651\t- `clear(): void`: Kill every particle and free the emitters (GPU buffers, materials).\n652\t- `dispose(): void`\n653\t\n654\t\n655\t\n656\t### class Breakables\n657\t\n658\t- `new Breakables(ctx: Ctx)`\n659\t- `readonly blocks: Breakable[]`\n660\t- `readonly tiles: Crumble[]`\n661\t- `get chunks: number`: Chunks alive now (tests, HUDs).\n662\t- `add(o: BreakableOptions): Breakable`\n663\t- `restore(b: Breakable): void`: Put a broken block back, whole (its old pieces dissolve as usual).\n664\t- `byCollider(handle: number): Breakable | undefined`: The block whose collider this is (a punch's ray hit, a projectile).\n665\t- `near(at: Vec3, reach: number): Breakable | undefined`: The unbroken block nearest to `at` within `reach` of its surface (a punch, a blast radius).\n666\t- `break(b: Breakable, at?: Vec3, speed = 5): void`: Shatter a block: chunks fly away from `at` (default its middle) at up to `speed` m/s.\n667\t- `crumble(o: CrumbleOptions): Crumble`: A floor tile that drops shortly after you step on it, and grows back.\n668\t- `update(dt: number, standing = -1): void`: Per frame: tiles under `standing` (a collider handle: the character's `groundCollider`) start to shake, shaking tiles drop, dropped ones grow back; old chunks dissolve away.\n669\t\n670\t\n671\t### validateClip()\n672\t\n673\t```ts\n674\tfunction validateClip(clip: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec): string[]\n675\t```\n676\t\n677\tProblems with a clip definition itself (unknown joints, bad key order, loop mismatch).\n678\t\n679\t\n680\t### analyzeClip()\n681\t\n682\t```ts\n683\tfunction analyzeClip(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip): ClipReport\n684\t```\n685\t\n686\t\n687\t### class Tweens\n688\t\n689\tA set of running tweens (the engine keeps one per game: `ctx.tweens`).\n690\t\n691\t- `get count: number`: Tweens still running.\n692\t- `to(target: T, to: Partial<Record<keyof T, number>>, options: TweenOptions = {}): Tween`: Glide `target`'s numeric properties to `to`. A new tween on the same target and property takes over (the old one stops where it is).\n693\t- `value(from: number, to: number, options: TweenOptions, apply: (v: number) => void): Tween`: Tween a plain number from `from` to `to`, calling `apply(v)` every frame.\n694\t- `call(seconds: number, fn: () => void): Tween`: Call `fn` after `seconds` of game time.\n695\t- `cancel(target?: object): void`: Stop every tween on `target` (or everything).\n696\t- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (level unload). Pending `done` promises resolve.\n697\t- `update(dt: number): void`\n698\t\n699\t\n700\t### glowMaterial()\n701\t\n702\t```ts\n703\tfunction glowMaterial(color: ColorRepresentation): MeshBasicNodeMaterial\n704\t```\n705\t\n706\tUnlit, full-bright material for a colour (flames, eyes, screens, crystal cores), shared and\n707\tcached per colour like `toonMaterial` (`userData.shared`; disposing it drops it from the\n708\tcache). Its vertices snap with the toon materials' under the `ps1` filter, so a glowing part\n709\tstays on its model.\n710\t\n711\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76d12802-c986-437e-adae-1ae347966275","uuid":"a238e6e9-e63d-44a7-8245-4e8f5f027e9f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:38.756Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-162048--xa8053f-work/76d12802-c986-437e-adae-1ae347966275/tool-results/bzqcfplkg.txt","content":"\n### class Engine\n\n- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listeners, no AudioContext (`audio.counts` still counts), no panels; errors go to `engin…\n- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throw…\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n- `readonly context: GameContext`\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n- `readonly particles: Particles`\n- `readonly hud: Hud`\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\n- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n- `frame: number`\n- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n- `time: number`\n- `readonly renderer: View`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\n- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\n- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\n- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\n- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\n- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\n- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manua…\n- `snapshot(): Promise<Snapshot>`: The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the game's own state (`Game.snapshot`). Plain JSON. Taken once the las…\n- `restore(saved: Snapshot): Promise<void>`: Put the running level back to a snapshot of it: the same game (by name), already loaded; nothing in between is replayed. The level's recording starts again from it (`Recording.from`), so what is played next replays with `replay()`. Live input held now is let…\n- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the simulation: game time, every physics body (exact), `ctx.random`'s state and the game's own (`Game.fingerprint`). Two runs that should be the same (a replay) compare equal. Not the `status()` line: a debug line may carry measurements (a step's mi…\n\n\n### interface EngineOptions\n\n- `container?: HTMLElement`\n- `resolution?: Resolution`\n- `aspect?: AspectMode`: `adaptive` (default): the art height stays `resolution.height` and the art width follows the screen's aspect (portrait phones fill the screen). `fixed`: always exactly `resolution`, letterboxed. `fill`: adjusts both art dimensions to cover the viewport at an…\n- `maxFps?: number`: Cap the render loop (simulation keeps its fixed 60 Hz physics step). Default 60, so 120 Hz phones don't run the whole pipeline twice per game frame. 0 = display rate.\n- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow map quality, or `auto` (default: low on touch devices, else medium; lowered once on a slow start).\n- `loadingScreen?: boolean`: Pixel-styled loading indicator while the renderer, physics and assets load. Default true.\n- `mode?: RenderMode`\n- `edges?: EdgeSettings`\n- `camera?: CameraConfig`: Camera preset + parameters. Pick one per game; not meant to change during play.\n- `filters?: readonly string[]`: Post filters (ids from FILTERS) applied in Pixel mode, in order.\n- `look?: Look`: A full look: pixel art or clean rendering and filters per layer (characters & objects, environment) plus whole-scene filters (render/look.ts). Wins over `filters` and `edges`.\n- `seed?: number`: Seed for `ctx.random`: each level gets its own stream from this and the game's name, so the same seed replays the same game. Default 1. `?seed=` in the URL.\n- `record?: number`: Frames of input the engine keeps for each level (`recording()`). Default `MAX_FRAMES`, an hour at 60 fps (up to some 20 MB of JSON with the mouse moving all the time); 0 records nothing.\n- `debugUI?: boolean`: Debug panel. Default: on in dev (`vite`) or with ?debug=1, off in production builds.\n- `debugKeys?: DebugKeysOption`: Engine hotkeys (P Pixel/Raw, R resolution, ` debug panel, [ ] looks, M mute): developer tools. Default: on with the debug panel (dev, ?debug=1), off in a shipped game, whose players have the same options in the settings. `true` / `false`: always on / off; an…\n- `settings?: { button?: TouchCorner | boolean; key?: string | false }`: The player's settings (`engine.settings`): look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate, sound and debug info, in a panel the ⚙ button opens. `button`: its corner (default, or true: 'top-right'; on a mouse it shows while the pointer moves), or false for n…\n- `touch?: boolean`: On-screen joystick + buttons. Default: auto (coarse pointer), or ?touch=1 / ?touch=0.\n- `touchButtons?: readonly TouchButton[]`: On-screen action buttons. Default: the platformer's (DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS).\n- `touchBar?: boolean`: The touch top bar of the engine hotkeys (ⓘ debug panel, P, R, ◐ looks, ♪ mute), on a touch screen while they are on (see `debugKeys`). Default true; false hides it even then, unless the debug panel is on (`?debug=1`, dev builds).\n- `background?: number`\n\n\n### interface Game\n\nA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\nlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (`npm run replay`, src/games.…\n- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is…\n- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay mu…\n- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\n- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `re…\n- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in first person).\n- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, timers, listeners, HMR hooks.\n\n\n### interface GameContext\n\n- `readonly engine: Engine`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n\n\n### interface HeadlessOptions\n\n`Engine.headless`: the page's options (those about the page do nothing), and where it runs.\n\n- `viewport?: Viewport`: The viewport the art resolution and framing come from, as a page's would (default 960×540 at DPR 1). Pointer aim and HUD layout depend on it: a replay uses its recording's (`Recording.view`).\n- `assets?: AssetReader`: How models and packs are read: `nodeAssets()` (src/engine/node.ts) reads `public/`.\n\n\n### class Settings\n\n`engine.settings`: the player's settings, applied to the engine and saved. A field the page URL\nsets (`?look=`, `?mode=`, `?res=`, `?quality=`, `?fps=`, `?debug=`: review flags) wins over a\nsaved one at start (which stays saved). `open`, `close` and `toggle` show and hide the\nsettings panel.\n\n- `new Settings(host: SettingsHost, key: string | null)`\n- `readonly key: string | null`\n- `get values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>`: The player's choices in effect: fields left out are the game's (or a review flag's).\n- `get isOpen: boolean`\n- `set(patch: PlayerSettings): void`: Change some settings: applied at once and saved. A field set to `undefined` goes back to the game's.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget every choice, the held ones too: the game's own picture again.\n- `replace(values: PlayerSettings): void`: Make `values` the settings, changing only the fields that differ: the ones it leaves out go back to the game's.\n- `onChange(fn: (values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>) => void): () => void`: Called with the values after every change; returns the unsubscribe.\n- `open(): void`\n- `close(): void`\n- `toggle(): void`\n\n\n### class CameraRig\n\nBase rig: owns the three.js camera, zoom and the ground-plane movement basis.\n\n- `new CameraRig(config: CameraConfig)`\n- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera | PerspectiveCamera`\n- `readonly focus: Vector3`: World point the rig is centred on (used for shadow framing).\n- `zoom: number`\n- `readonly minZoom: number`\n- `readonly maxZoom: number`\n- `readonly zoomable: boolean`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n- `readonly hidesTarget: boolean`: First person hides the player model.\n- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n- `controlsCharacter: boolean`: False while the free camera is flying (input drives the camera, not the player).\n- `snap: boolean`: Pixel-snap the camera (ortho only).\n- `aspect: number`: Viewport aspect (width / height), following the art resolution (see `setAspect`).\n- `rayAt(x: number, y: number, origin: Vector3, dir: Vector3): void`: The ray through screen point (x, y) in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\n- `setZoom(zoom: number): void`\n- `setAspect(aspect: number): void`: Match the art resolution's aspect (adaptive framing changes it with the screen). `update()` calls this from `CameraUpdate.resolution`; the engine also calls it on swap.\n- `teleport(target: Vector3): void`: Place the camera without smoothing.\n- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3 }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n- `update(u: CameraUpdate): void`\n- `describe(): Record<string, unknown>`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n- `state(): CameraRigState`: The rig as data, exact (`engine.snapshot()`).\n- `setState(s: CameraRigState): void`: Put the rig back where `state()` found it (the same preset).\n\n\n### class ThirdPersonRig\n\nOver-the-shoulder orbit camera: mouse-drag or Q/E to orbit, wheel to zoom, avoids walls.\n\n- `new ThirdPersonRig(config: CameraConfig = {})`\n- `readonly preset: \"third\"`\n- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera`\n- `yaw: number`\n- `pitch: number`\n- `describe(): { yaw: number; pitch: number; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n\n\n### interface LightRequestOptions\n\n- `position?: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]`: World position (copied). Ignored while `follow` is set (then it is `follow` + `offset`).\n- `follow?: Object3D | null`: Follow an object's world position every frame (a projectile, a monster, the hero).\n- `offset?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Offset from `follow` (world axes).\n- `color?: ColorRepresentation`\n- `intensity?: number`: Point light intensity at full strength (before flicker and fades). Default 4.\n- `radius?: number`: Reach in metres (PointLight.distance: light is 0 beyond it). Default 6.\n- `decay?: number`: Distance falloff exponent (PointLight.decay; a uniform, free to change). Default 1.2.\n- `flicker?: FlickerPreset`\n- `priority?: number`: Importance multiplier: the hero's light 4, a loot beam 2, a torch 1 (default).\n- `fadeIn?: number`: Fade times in seconds (defaults 0.25 in, 0.2 out).\n- `fadeOut?: number`\n- `lifetime?: number`: Release automatically after this many seconds (spell flashes, explosions).\n- `name?: string`: Optional label for debugging (`pool.describe()`).\n\n\n### class CharacterController\n\nKinematic capsule driven by Rapier's KinematicCharacterController, with classic\nplatformer affordances (coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height).\n\nThe body moves in continuous world space; the visual model is interpolated by\nPhysics.bind and only *looks* pixel-aligned through the render pipeline.\n\n- `new CharacterController(physics: Physics, options: CharacterOptions)`\n- `readonly body: RAPIER.RigidBody`\n- `readonly collider: RAPIER.Collider`\n- `readonly controller: RAPIER.KinematicCharacterController`\n- `readonly velocity: Vector3`\n- `grounded: boolean`\n- `facing: number`\n- `get feet: Vector3`: Feet position (bottom of the capsule), continuous world space.\n- `interpolatedFeet(alpha: number): Vector3`: Feet position blended between the previous and current physics step (render use).\n- `setInput(move: Vector3, jumpPressed: boolean, jumpHeld: boolean): void`: Set desired horizontal move direction (world space, length ≤ 1) and jump input.\n- `teleport(position: [number, number, number]): void`\n- `fixedUpdate(dt: number): void`: Call from Physics.update's fixedUpdate callback.\n- `attachAnimations(model: Object3D, clips: readonly AnimationClip[]): void`: Attach a model's animation clips (expects Idle / Walk / Jump).\n- `get animation: CharacterAnim`\n- `updateVisual(model: Object3D, dt: number): void`: Per render frame: pick animation state and advance the mixer.\n\n\n### class Input\n\nKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n  a frame is not lost.\n- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n\n- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n\n\n\n### class Hud\n\nPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\nthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\nlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n\nRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\nresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n\n  ctx.hud.clear();\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n\n- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n- `visible: boolean`\n- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n- `get height: number`\n- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n\n\n### class AudioManager\n\nWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\nsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\npersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n\nBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\nfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\nwas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n\n- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n- `context: AudioContext | null`\n- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n- `get muted: boolean`\n- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n- `stopMusic(): void`\n- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n\n\n### interface SoundDef\n\n- `wave: Wave`\n- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n- `sustain?: number`\n- `decay?: number`\n- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n- `arpRate?: number`\n- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n\n\n### interface Song\n\nA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\nwhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\nEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\nnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n\n- `bpm: number`\n- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n\n\n\n### class Timeline\n\n- `new Timeline(o: TimelineOptions)`\n- `readonly duration: number`\n- `time: number`: The playhead (s).\n- `playing: boolean`\n- `speed: number`: Playback rate (1: real time, 0.5: slow motion).\n- `ended: boolean`: True once the end has been reached (by playing or skipping).\n- `readonly fired: string[]`: Cue names in the order they fired (tests and tools).\n- `play(): void`\n- `pause(): void`\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Advance by `dt` seconds of game time (times `speed`) while playing, firing the cues passed.\n- `seek(t: number): void`: Move the playhead to `t` without firing the cues in between (scrubbing). Cues before `t` count as done and cues after it will fire again (going back re-arms them). Ignored while skipping (a cue that loops back cannot undo a skip).\n- `skip(): void`: Jump to the end: the cues not yet fired run (except the cosmetic ones), then `onEnd`. Cues after `duration` never fire, played or skipped.\n- `camera(t = this.time): CameraShot | null`: Where the camera is at `t` (default now): on each key at its time, gliding between keys on a smooth curve (Catmull-Rom tangents, so the speed has no jolt at a key; the first and last key of each shot ease in and out). A `cut` key starts a new shot. Null witho…\n- `line(t = this.time): Line | null`: The subtitle showing at `t` (default now), or null.\n- `bars(t = this.time): number`: How far the letterbox bars are in (0..1) at `t` (default now): sliding in at the start, out at the end.\n\n\n### interface TimelineOptions\n\n- `shots?: readonly CameraKey[]`\n- `cues?: readonly Cue[]`\n- `lines?: readonly Line[]`\n- `duration?: number`: Length (s); default the last key, cue or line.\n- `bars?: number`: Seconds the letterbox bars take to slide in and out (default 0.6; 0: no bars).\n- `onEnd?: () => void`: Called once when the playhead reaches the end (or the scene is skipped).\n\n\n### class NavGrid\n\n- `new NavGrid(width: number, height: number, o: NavGridOptions = {})`\n- `static fromRows(rows: readonly string[], o: NavGridOptions & { blocked?: string } = {}): NavGrid`: From an ASCII map: `blocked` characters are closed, everything else open. Default '# ': walls and pits (a ' ' has no floor in RoomKit's maps); past the end of a short row is a wall.\n- `readonly width: number`\n- `readonly height: number`\n- `readonly cell: number`\n- `readonly ox: number`: World x, z of cell (0, 0)'s corner.\n- `readonly oz: number`\n- `readonly open: Uint8Array`: 1 = walkable.\n- `version: number`: Bumped by `setOpen`: flow fields rebuild.\n- `flowBuilds: number`: How many times a flow field was built (tools: should be one per target cell change).\n- `col(x: number): number`\n- `row(z: number): number`\n- `centerX(col: number): number`: World x, z of a cell's centre.\n- `centerZ(row: number): number`\n- `isOpen(col: number, row: number): boolean`\n- `walkable(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is the world point on a walkable cell?\n- `setOpen(col: number, row: number, open: boolean): void`\n- `path(from: readonly [number, number, number], to: readonly [number, number, number]): [number, number, number][] | null`: Shortest path between two world points (x, z used; y kept from `from`): A* over cells, no corner cutting, then smoothed so it only turns where a wall is in the way. Null when there is no way. The first point is `from`, the last `to` (snapped into its cell).\n- `smooth(pts: [number, number, number][]): [number, number, number][]`: Drop every point the path can skip in a straight line.\n- `lineOfSight(ax: number, az: number, bx: number, bz: number, radius = 0): boolean`: Can you see (or walk straight) from a to b? Walks the cells under the segment (a DDA); `radius` > 0 also tests two lines offset sideways (a body that wide fits).\n- `castWall(ax: number, az: number, dx: number, dz: number, max: number): number`: Distance from a along a direction to the first closed cell (at most `max`): the same cell walk as `lineOfSight`, so a vision cone drawn with it shows exactly what a guard can see.\n- `flowTo(x: number, z: number): boolean`: A flow field toward the world point (x, z): the walking distance from every cell to it. Rebuilt only when the target changes cell or the grid changed (`version`). Returns whether it was rebuilt.\n- `flowDistance(x: number, z: number): number`: Walking distance to the flow target from a world point (Infinity: no way).\n- `flowDirection(x: number, z: number, out: [number, number]): boolean`: Which way to go from (x, z) toward the flow target: the unit direction to the lowest-distance neighbour (into `out` [x, z]). Returns false at the target or with no way.\n\n\n### class Ragdoll\n\n- `new Ragdoll(physics: Physics, root: Object3D, parts: readonly RagdollPart[], o: RagdollOptions = {})`\n- `readonly parts: readonly RagdollPart[]`\n- `readonly o: Required<RagdollOptions>`\n- `readonly root: Object3D`\n- `get active: boolean`: Whether the bodies exist (a `physics.clear()`, a level unload, removes them: then it is off).\n- `get bodies: RAPIER.RigidBody[]`: The bodies (root part first), while active.\n- `enable(o: { velocity?: Vec3; spin?: Vec3 } = {}): void`: Make the bodies from the model's current pose, moving at `velocity` (and spinning at `spin`, rad/s).\n- `push(at: Vec3 | Vector3, velocity: Vec3): void`: Knock the part nearest `at`: it gains `velocity` (m/s, applied there as an impulse of its own mass, so the same push works whatever the ragdoll's density), and its joints pass some of it on.\n- `mass(): number`: Total mass of the parts (world units), while active.\n- `speed(): number`: Fastest part (m/s): settled when this stays low.\n- `rootPose(out = new Vector3()): { at: Vector3; faceUp: boolean; heading: number }`: Where the root part (the pelvis) is and how it lies: face up (on its back: its forward, +Z, points up) or down, and the heading (yaw) a character lying like that has: on its back its head is behind it (−Z), on its front ahead (+Z). That is the heading to get…\n- `sync(): void`: Write the bodies into the joints (between the last two physics steps). Call every frame after the physics update.\n- `release(o: { ground?: number | ((x: number, z: number) => number); heading?: number; offset?: Vec3 } = {}): { apply(w: number): void }`: Remove the bodies and hand the pose back. The model root moves under the root part (feet at `ground`: a height, or a function of x, z called once the bodies are gone; turned to `heading`, default `rootPose().heading`) without moving the joints in the world; t…\n- `disable(): void`: Remove the bodies (the joints keep the last pose).\n\n\n### class WaterSurface\n\n- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n- `waves: Wave[]`\n- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n\n\n### class Precipitation\n\n- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n\n\n### groundFog()\n\n```ts\nfunction groundFog(o: { color?: number; top?: number; amount?: number; near?: number; far?: number } = {}): { node: Node; color: UniformNode<\"color\", Color>; amount: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; top: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; }\n```\n\nFog for `scene.fogNode` with live settings (uniforms: changing them never recompiles):\nground mist that thickens below `top` (m) and a haze that grows with distance from the\ncamera, both up to `amount` (0..1), in `color` (match the sky).\n\n  const mist = groundFog({ color: s.sky, top: 1.5 });\n  (scene as { fogNode: unknown }).fogNode = mist.node;\n  mist.amount.value = 0.8; mist.color.value.setHex(s.sky);\n\n\n### applySky()\n\n```ts\nfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n```\n\nPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n\n\n### skyAt()\n\n```ts\nfunction skyAt(hour: number): SkyState\n```\n\nThe sky at `hour` (wraps around 24).\n\n\n### compileClips()\n\n```ts\nfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n```\n\n\n### gaitClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction gaitClip(rig: RigSpec, g: GaitSpec): ClipDef\n```\n\n\n### interface ClipDef\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n- `loop?: boolean`\n- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instead of sinking.\n- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n- `notes?: string`\n\n\n\n### class SpringChain\n\nA chain of points hanging off a moving root. Call `update(dt, root, basis)` every frame\nwith the root's world position and its orientation (three columns: right, up, forward),\nthen read `points` (root first).\n\n- `new SpringChain(o: SpringChainOptions)`\n- `readonly points: Vec3[]`\n- `o: Required<SpringChainOptions>`\n- `reset(root: Vec3, basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): void`: Put the chain straight out in its rest pose (no swing).\n- `update(dt: number, root: Vec3, basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): void`\n- `swing(basis: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3]): number`: How far the tip is from where the rest pose puts it (m): how much it is swinging.\n\n\n### class Decals\n\n- `new Decals(scene: Scene, o: { capacity?: number } = {})`\n- `readonly meshes: Map<\"ring\" | \"crack\" | \"footprint\" | \"scorch\" | \"splat\", InstancedMesh<BufferGeometry<NormalBufferAttributes, BufferGeometryEventMap>, Material<...> | Material…`\n- `readonly o: { capacity?: number }`\n- `add(shape: DecalShape, at: Vector3Like, normal: Vector3Like, o: DecalOptions = {}): number`: Lay a mark at `at` on a surface facing `normal`. Returns its index in that shape's pool.\n- `count(shape?: DecalShape): number`: Marks alive (per shape or all).\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Per frame: age the marks; old ones dither away over a second.\n- `clear(): void`: Remove every mark (the meshes stay for reuse).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n\n### spatialize()\n\n```ts\nfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n```\n\nGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n\n\n### renderSound()\n\n```ts\nfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n```\n\nRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n\n\n### parseSong()\n\n```ts\nfunction parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong\n```\n\nFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n\n\n\n### class Particles\n\nPixel-art particles: CPU-simulated pools, drawn as camera-facing square sprites in flat\npalette colors. One emitter (one instanced draw call, one pooled instance buffer) per\npreset; sizes are whole art pixels, so in Pixel mode a particle is a crisp N×N block.\nAvailable as `ctx.particles`; emitters are created on first use and freed by `clear()`\n(the engine calls it when a level unloads).\n\n  ctx.particles.burst('dust', hero.feet);\n  ctx.particles.burst('skid', feet, { direction: [-vx, 0.3, -vz], count: 2 });\n\n- `new Particles(view: () => ParticleView)`\n- `readonly group: Group<Object3DEventMap>`: Engine-owned group holding every emitter.\n- `readonly presets: Map<string, ParticlePreset>`: Preset registry: the built-ins plus anything registered.\n- `readonly pixelWorld: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`: World units per art pixel at the camera focus (updated every frame).\n- `register(name: string, preset: ParticlePreset): void`: Add or replace a named preset. Replacing frees the old preset's emitter.\n- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is full, 0 for an unknown preset). Inline preset objects share one emitter per distinct content, so `burst({ ...literal }, at)` every…\n- `get alive: number`: Particles alive right now, over all emitters.\n- `get emitterCount: number`: Emitters (= draw calls at most) currently allocated.\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Simulate and upload. Called by the engine every frame (frozen while paused).\n- `reseed(rng: Rng): void`: Take bursts' randomness from `rng` (the engine passes each level's `ctx.random` fork).\n- `clear(): void`: Kill every particle and free the emitters (GPU buffers, materials).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n\n\n### class Breakables\n\n- `new Breakables(ctx: Ctx)`\n- `readonly blocks: Breakable[]`\n- `readonly tiles: Crumble[]`\n- `get chunks: number`: Chunks alive now (tests, HUDs).\n- `add(o: BreakableOptions): Breakable`\n- `restore(b: Breakable): void`: Put a broken block back, whole (its old pieces dissolve as usual).\n- `byCollider(handle: number): Breakable | undefined`: The block whose collider this is (a punch's ray hit, a projectile).\n- `near(at: Vec3, reach: number): Breakable | undefined`: The unbroken block nearest to `at` within `reach` of its surface (a punch, a blast radius).\n- `break(b: Breakable, at?: Vec3, speed = 5): void`: Shatter a block: chunks fly away from `at` (default its middle) at up to `speed` m/s.\n- `crumble(o: CrumbleOptions): Crumble`: A floor tile that drops shortly after you step on it, and grows back.\n- `update(dt: number, standing = -1): void`: Per frame: tiles under `standing` (a collider handle: the character's `groundCollider`) start to shake, shaking tiles drop, dropped ones grow back; old chunks dissolve away.\n\n\n### validateClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction validateClip(clip: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec): string[]\n```\n\nProblems with a clip definition itself (unknown joints, bad key order, loop mismatch).\n\n\n### analyzeClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction analyzeClip(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip): ClipReport\n```\n\n\n### class Tweens\n\nA set of running tweens (the engine keeps one per game: `ctx.tweens`).\n\n- `get count: number`: Tweens still running.\n- `to(target: T, to: Partial<Record<keyof T, number>>, options: TweenOptions = {}): Tween`: Glide `target`'s numeric properties to `to`. A new tween on the same target and property takes over (the old one stops where it is).\n- `value(from: number, to: number, options: TweenOptions, apply: (v: number) => void): Tween`: Tween a plain number from `from` to `to`, calling `apply(v)` every frame.\n- `call(seconds: number, fn: () => void): Tween`: Call `fn` after `seconds` of game time.\n- `cancel(target?: object): void`: Stop every tween on `target` (or everything).\n- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (level unload). Pending `done` promises resolve.\n- `update(dt: number): void`\n\n\n### glowMaterial()\n\n```ts\nfunction glowMaterial(color: ColorRepresentation): MeshBasicNodeMaterial\n```\n\nUnlit, full-bright material for a colour (flames, eyes, screens, crystal cores), shared and\ncached per colour like `toonMaterial` (`userData.shared`; disposing it drops it from the\ncache). Its vertices snap with the toon materials' under the `ps1` filter, so a glowing part\nstays on its model.\n\n","numLines":711,"startLine":1,"totalLines":711}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011zW3vwesTRNbYPQm2F3kKP","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
