{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgknMDrDGTWne8cMXpc7n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# President Evil 4 is finished\n\nThe game is complete and everything `TASK.md` asks for is in this folder, but no person has played it yet. A bot plays the whole story from arrival to credits on Standard with no cheats, in about 40 game minutes; on one of its runs it died seven times and retried each time.\n\n**Tests:** the full suite (`node tools/test.mjs`) passed 592 of 592. I then made one small co-op fix, so client copies now show thrown bottles, nets and harpoons. After that I reran only the co-op tests, all four AI Hub harness scenarios, and the party and solo browser tests, and they all pass. To run the suite again, first run `npm install` in the dev folder, because I removed `node_modules`.\n\n**Nothing from the plan's cut list was cut.** That includes the Launch, WebRTC invite codes, the Dame's crypt, the causeway rowboats and the Claim Notes.\n\n**What still needs a person, or differs from the plan:**\n- **Feel and balance:** these were tuned only against the bot and screenshots, so expect to adjust them with the dev sliders. Getting the bot through also meant softening some attacks and adding pickups, so the numbers no longer all match the plan.\n- **Hardware:** the controller, touch and sound checks used stand-ins (a stubbed gamepad, a phone profile and audio level readings). No real pad, phone or speakers were involved.\n- **Simplified from the plan:**\n    - Nave columns only show a shatter effect and stay solid.\n    - The Bosun's crate stacks don't break; he throws his anchor over them instead.\n    - The launch finale moves the world past the boat rather than following a spline.\n    - A harpoon adds the 3 seconds of kneel by hitting the Bosun, not the chain.\n    - The optional Kenney art packs aren't used.\n    - The crypt knights got a telegraphed swing and slower hunting.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:48\n- **Source:** Resident Evil 4\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** third-person action-horror shooter with crowd control, resource management and an escort; over-the-shoulder with a laser sight, letterboxed cinematic cameras, one on-rails shooting finale\n- **Tech:** Riftlight pixel-engine 0.46.0 (three.js r186 WebGPU, Rapier), unmodified; plain JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild; AI Hub session client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel style at 480×270 with a soft vignette; palette toon materials, data-driven puppets and props, library props for generic things\n- **Animation:** engine clip system on the hero rig, with masked upper and lower layers, about 60 new clips and gaits, a spring-chain scarf and ragdolls\n- **Audio:** all code, no files; ten original songs with music at 10% by default, about 60 synthesised sound effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices per speaker\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 shared-world co-op for 1–4 players; standalone parties over BroadcastChannel and WebRTC invite codes with a public STUN server\n- **Testing:** headless Node scripts and Playwright browsers behind one command, covering a scripted whole-game playthrough, unit and AI suites, stubbed gamepad, phone profile, Web Audio levels, two-page parties and the AI Hub harness; no subagents for review, no human playtest\n- **Research:** read the design plan, the engine kit docs, and the AI Hub protocol README and example; no web research on the source\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 35 real issues (my estimate): roughly 20 from the bot playthrough, 3 from the Node suites, 12 from browser and harness runs. Bugs in the bot or the tests themselves aren't counted.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** About 12:\n    - Reloading a checkpoint lost nested story scripts.\n    - Set pieces changed shared level data, which broke retries.\n    - A checkpoint taken mid-vault put you inside a wall.\n    - Gate opening crashed.\n    - The road had no door action, so there was no way forward.\n    - The final boss valves couldn't be hit.\n    - Launch mines sat under the ground collider.\n    - Opening the Case with Triangle dropped your gun.\n    - Esc and I opened a menu and shut it in the same frame.\n    - Menu taps were lost at low frame rates.\n    - Hub traffic starved when rendering was slow.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The whole-game playthrough in Node. It runs about 60× real time, so a full game took under a minute, and it found most of the game-breaking bugs.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The per-sound render checks and the model checks. They guard against typos but never found a gameplay problem.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** `Engine.headless`: the same game code runs in Node, deterministic and very fast, so a bot can play the whole game. A close second is writing models, textures and clips as plain data.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A few times:\n    - One model id (`armchair`) clashed with the library.\n    - `shelf` is actually `bookshelf`.\n    - `TEXTURE_LIBRARY.get` throws rather than returning null.\n    - Pad presses are dropped at time scale 0.\n    - A full-size ground collider silently blocked shots at things below y=0.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The Intent layer with the shared bot and the story stage system; attract mode and the playthrough test hang off them. After that, over-the-shoulder aiming with hit zones built from joint positions.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Frame and input timing at low frame rates in software WebGPU: which presses survive, when a level swap really happens, and what runs at time scale 0.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None. The engine's hash matches the kit's.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Let gamepad presses reach `wasPressed` at time scale 0, as keyboard presses do. An official shoulder-offset third-person rig would also help.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The plan is a complete design with verified engine facts, real numbers and a real script, so effort went into building rather than guessing. The originality guardrails were clear too.\n- **What was confusing?** The Online line is only a link until the plan explains it. The plan also lists more animations and props than one vertical slice strictly needs, so I had to judge where a shared clip was good enough.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Keeping the systems working together across a long linear game: the Dame, crowd AI, checkpoints and set pieces interact, and most serious bugs were at those joins. I also couldn't judge game feel with a pad in hand.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Ask for a short feel checklist a script can approximate, and say up front which listed assets are required and which are optional.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162048__xa8053f/work`:\n- `PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162048.html`: the game; open it in a WebGPU browser such as a recent Chrome or Edge\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162048/`: source, tests and `docs/CONTENT.md`, which explains how to add areas, enemies and items"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":855,"cache_read_input_tokens":755774,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":855},"output_tokens":724,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76d12802-c986-437e-adae-1ae347966275","uuid":"0cd3ab22-e490-4355-a5c9-1f9b648f3a24","timestamp":"2026-11-06T01:10:29.756Z","request_id":"req_011CgknMDYrXyLqaAmpDWLUm"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: Not satisfied: PLAN.md's Section 15 and Section 17 call for testing that is only partly done, and the transcript shows gaps. The Browser testing list (Section 15) asks for a Firefox and WebKit check, and the transcript shows the no-WebGPU message check ran, but the 'Before finishing' captures of each mood, the Case, the Shop, and menus at 1920x1080 and 4:3 were only partially captured (not every mood, and no 4:3 capture of menus was shown). Section 17 requires a Testing Feedback answer count from actual testing, which the final reply presents as an estimate ('About 35 real issues (my estimate)'). Also, the reply itself states 'no person has played it yet' and that tuning was done only against the bot, which is acceptable since no human-only step is possible here, but the plan's Done list requires that every enemy, weapon and item in the data be reachable in play, and the transcript does not show verification of that. Finally, the 'Done' condition for TASK.md is met (the HTML and exit-interview.json are in the folder), but the plan-level completion is not clearly demonstrated."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76d12802-c986-437e-adae-1ae347966275","uuid":"22af5123-91e0-4832-843e-80ab1fb7ab14","timestamp":"2026-11-06T01:10:47.650Z","isSynthetic":true}
