{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VjozAYKsndCdeBAZp3n6Rg","type":"tool_result","content":"1142 passed, 0 failed\n\n[exited with code 0]\n0\nPLAN.md PROGRESS.md PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162048 PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162048.html STAGE.md TASK.md exit-interview.json \n80408b4c72ef08be\ndate: 2026-11-05 16:20:48\nsource: Resident Evil 4\nplan: PLAN.md\ngenre: Third-person action-horror shooter with crowd control, resource management and an escort\nperspective: Over-the-shoulder third person with a laser sight; letterboxed cinematic cameras; one on-rails shooting finale\ntech: Riftlight pixel-engine 0.46.0 (three.js r186 WebGPU, Rapier), plain JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild, AI Hub session client 1.4.0\nartDirection: The engine native pixel style at 480 by 270 with a soft vignette: palette toon materials, data-driven puppets and props, plus library props for generic things\nanimation: Engine clip system on the hero rig: masked upper and lower layers, about 60 new clips and gaits, spring-chain scarf, ragdolls, head look\naudio: All code, no files. Ten original songs as data with music at 10 percent by default, about 60 synthesised sound effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices per speaker\nonline: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 shared-world co-op (1 to 4); standalone parties over BroadcastChannel and WebRTC invite codes with a public STUN server\ntesting: Headless node scripts and Playwright browsers on this computer, behind one command (node tools/test.mjs): a scripted whole-game playthrough, unit and AI suites, stubbed gamepad, phone profile, Web Audio levels, two-page parties, the AI Hub harness. The game itself, the AI Hub harness and a stubbed touch-only device also ran in Firefox with WebGPU forced on, a virtual display and a null audio sink. WebKit has no WebGPU here, so it shows the clear message. No subagents for review. No human playtest.\nresearch: Read the design plan, the engine kit guide, API and assets docs, and the AI Hub protocol README and example. No web research on the source.\nenvironment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\nmodel: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\nusage: added on import\n52, itemised in docs/TESTING.md: 21 from the whole-game bot playthrough, 9 from the node suites, 11 from browser and harness runs, 10 from captures, 1 from checking the code against the plan. Bugs in the bot or the tests themselves are not counted, and how to split or merge an issue is a judgement call.\n15. For example: reloading a checkpoint left the game stuck; set pieces changed shared level data and broke every retry; a mid-vault checkpoint put you inside a wall; the road had no door action; the final boss could not be hurt; launch mines could not be shot; Night Shift crashed on its first frame; opening the Case with Triangle dropped your gun; Esc opened Pause and shut it in the same frame; hub traffic starved under slow rendering, and a host leaving early could fail the hub check.\nThe scripted whole-game playthrough in Node. It runs about 60 times faster than real time, so one run of the entire game took under a minute, and it found most of the game-breaking bugs.\nThe per-sound render checks. They stayed green after the first run. They guard against typos, but never found a gameplay problem.\nEngine.headless. The same game code runs in Node with no GPU, deterministic and very fast, so a bot can play the whole game. A close second is writing models, textures and clips as plain data.\nA few times. One model id clashed with the library (armchair) and the catalog threw. shelf is not an id, it is bookshelf. TEXTURE_LIBRARY.get throws rather than returning null. I also wrongly blamed dropped pad presses at time scale 0 for a menu bug that was my own; a direct test showed pad presses work there. And I wrongly concluded the game could not run in Firefox here, because my first attempt had no display. A full-size ground collider silently blocked shots at things below y = 0.\nThe Intent layer with the shared Bot and the story stage system. Everything else, from attract mode to the playthrough test, hangs off them. After that, the over-the-shoulder aiming with hit zones built from joint positions.\nWorking out frame and input timing at low frame rates in the software WebGPU browser here: which presses survive, when a level swap really happens, and what can run at time scale 0.\nnone\nAn official shoulder-offset third-person rig, so a game need not replace rig.step by hand.\nThe plan is a complete design with verified engine facts, real numbers and a real script. I could spend the effort on building instead of guessing. The originality guardrails were clear too.\nMostly the Online line, which is only a link, until the plan explained it. The plan also lists more animations and props than a single vertical slice strictly needs, so I had to judge where a shared clip was good enough.\nKeeping every system working together across a long linear game alone: the Dame, the crowd AI, checkpoints and set pieces all interact, and most of the serious bugs were at those joins. I could not judge game feel with hands on a pad here, only through the bot and screenshots.\nAsk for a short human-style feel checklist that a script can approximate, and say up front which listed assets are required and which are optional. A real person should still play it: balance here was only tuned against the bot.\n{\"name\":\"PRESIDENT EVIL 4\",\"tagline\":\"Kidnap and ransom, premium tier\"}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76d12802-c986-437e-adae-1ae347966275","uuid":"2fe6b937-af5d-4f24-96a5-86e43f379593","timestamp":"2026-11-06T02:40:50.605Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1142 passed, 0 failed\n\n[exited with code 0]\n0\nPLAN.md PROGRESS.md PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162048 PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-202