{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkuGj54MHAr2YMUSpC8q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# President Evil 4: final report\n\nThe game is finished and everything `TASK.md` asks for is in this folder. The full suite (`node tools/test.mjs`) passes **1142 of 1142** on the final code, in a single run covering Chromium and Firefox. **Nobody has played it yet**, so feel and balance are tuned only against the bot.\n\n## Checked against the plan's Done list\n| Done item | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | Source scan found only a false match (\"pa**thToDo**or\") |\n| Every menu item does what it says | Dev tools, pause, title and Settings driven through the real screens. This found one more bug, now fixed: restarting after a dev teleport crashed or loaded the wrong area |\n| Every enemy, weapon and item reachable | All 12 enemy types spawned in one bot run; every chest, pickup and shop item obtained through real prompts; all 6 notes read |\n| Single HTML file and dev folder | Both present; AI Hub client is the first script, unmodified |\n| Engine kept as shipped | Hash matches the kit's |\n| Testing | One command, including a whole-game bot playthrough and a real replay comparison |\n| AI Hub and standalone multiplayer | All 4 harness scenarios plus same-computer and WebRTC parties, in both browsers |\n| Dev mode, attract mode, glyphs, touch-only, music at 10% | Each has its own passing tests |\n| Temporary files and processes | Removed, none of mine still running |\n\n**Browsers:**\n- **Chromium:** everything passes.\n- **Firefox:** the game, harness and parties pass, but it needs WebGPU forced on and a virtual display. The touch-only test used a stubbed device, and music was measured through a silent audio device.\n- **WebKit:** no WebGPU even when launched directly with its feature on, so it shows the clear message the plan expects, with no page errors.\n\n**Different from the plan:** some attacks are softer and some pickups were added, including the crypt knights' telegraphed swing. The launch moves the world past the boat rather than following a spline. The optional Kenney packs aren't used.\n\n**What this computer can't check:** how it feels to play, real controllers, touch devices, speakers and TVs, real Safari or iOS, and Firefox with WebGPU on by default.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:48\n- **Source:** Resident Evil 4\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** third-person action-horror shooter with crowd control, resource management and an escort; over-the-shoulder with a laser sight, letterboxed cinematic cameras, one on-rails shooting finale\n- **Tech:** Riftlight pixel-engine 0.46.0 (three.js r186 WebGPU, Rapier), plain JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild, AI Hub session client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel style at 480×270 with a soft vignette; palette toon materials, data-driven puppets and props, library props for generic things\n- **Animation:** engine clip system on the hero rig, with masked upper and lower layers, about 60 new clips and gaits, a spring-chain scarf, ragdolls and head look\n- **Audio:** all code, no files; ten original songs with music at 10% by default, about 60 synthesised sound effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices per speaker\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 shared-world co-op for 1–4 players; standalone parties over BroadcastChannel and WebRTC invite codes with a public STUN server\n- **Testing:** headless Node scripts and Playwright browsers behind one command: a scripted whole-game playthrough, unit and AI suites, stubbed gamepad, phone profile, Web Audio levels, two-page parties, the AI Hub harness. The game, the harness and a stubbed touch-only device also ran in Firefox with WebGPU forced on, a virtual display and a null audio sink. WebKit has no WebGPU here, so it shows the clear message. No subagents for review. No human playtest.\n- **Research:** read the design plan, the engine kit guide, API and assets docs, and the AI Hub protocol README and example; no web research on the source\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 52, itemised in `docs/TESTING.md`: 21 from the whole-game bot playthrough, 9 from the Node suites, 11 from browser and harness runs, 10 from captures, 1 from checking the code against the plan. Bugs in the bot or the tests themselves aren't counted, and how to split or merge an issue is a judgement call.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 15. For example:\n    - Reloading a checkpoint left the game stuck.\n    - Set pieces changed shared level data and broke every retry.\n    - A mid-vault checkpoint put you inside a wall.\n    - The road had no door action.\n    - The final boss couldn't be hurt.\n    - Launch mines couldn't be shot.\n    - Night Shift crashed on its first frame.\n    - Opening the Case with Triangle dropped your gun.\n    - Esc opened Pause and shut it in the same frame.\n    - Hub traffic starved under slow rendering, and a host leaving early could fail the hub check.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted whole-game playthrough in Node. It runs about 60× real time, so a full game took under a minute, and it found most of the game-breaking bugs.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The per-sound render checks. They stayed green after the first run; they guard against typos but never found a gameplay problem.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** `Engine.headless`: the same game code runs in Node with no GPU, deterministic and very fast, so a bot can play the whole game. A close second is writing models, textures and clips as plain data.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A few times:\n    - One model id (`armchair`) clashed with the library and the catalog threw.\n    - `shelf` isn't an id; it's `bookshelf`.\n    - `TEXTURE_LIBRARY.get` throws rather than returning null.\n    - A full-size ground collider silently blocked shots at things below y=0.\n    - I wrongly blamed dropped pad presses at time scale 0 for a menu bug that was my own; a direct test showed pad presses work there.\n    - I wrongly concluded the game couldn't run in Firefox here, because my first attempt had no display.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The Intent layer with the shared bot and the story stage system; everything from attract mode to the playthrough test hangs off them. After that, over-the-shoulder aiming with hit zones built from joint positions.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Frame and input timing at low frame rates in software WebGPU: which presses survive, when a level swap really happens, and what can run at time scale 0.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** An official shoulder-offset third-person rig, so a game need not replace `rig.step` by hand.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The plan is a complete design with verified engine facts, real numbers and a real script, so effort went into building rather than guessing. The originality guardrails were clear too.\n- **What was confusing?** Mostly the Online line, which is only a link until the plan explains it. The plan also lists more animations and props than one vertical slice strictly needs, so I had to judge where a shared clip was good enough.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Keeping every system working together across a long linear game: the Dame, the crowd AI, checkpoints and set pieces all interact, and most serious bugs were at those joins. I also couldn't judge game feel with a pad in hand, only through the bot and screenshots.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Ask for a short human-style feel checklist that a script can approximate, and say up front which listed assets are required and which are optional. A real person should still play it, because balance was tuned only against the bot.\n\n## Files\nTo rerun the suite, first run `npm install` in the dev folder.\n\nEverything is in `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162048__xa8053f/work`:\n- `PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162048.html`: the game; open it in a recent Chrome or Edge\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162048/`: source and tests, with `docs/TESTING.md` (issue log) and `docs/CONTENT.md` (how to add areas, enemies and items)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":236,"cache_read_input_tokens":892191,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":236},"output_tokens":709,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76d12802-c986-437e-adae-1ae347966275","uuid":"0730ea15-0cb7-48ad-a547-20a92580ece7","timestamp":"2026-11-06T02:41:17.386Z","request_id":"req_011CgkuGijxfnaGXXFSvhVgb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: Not demonstrated. The transcript shows the deliverables (HTML, exit-interview.json, dev folder) and a 1142/1142 test run, but the condition requires everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer to be done. The transcript shows the game was not run in real WebKit or any real Safari/iOS, and Firefox runs only with WebGPU forced on and a virtual display. The final message also says real touch devices, controllers, speakers and TVs were only stubbed, and the Done list's 'every enemy, weapon and item reachable' was tested through a bot and sweep rather than a human play. Those steps are arguably beyond this computer, but the assistant itself notes several items as 'can't check' without showing an exhausted attempt. Also, the STAGE.md and PLAN.md Done list were printed only in a later turn, and no transcript evidence shows the full PLAN.md section 15 list was checked item-by-item against the final state."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76d12802-c986-437e-adae-1ae347966275","uuid":"2062b05e-558a-43e6-af8f-2746a1938f68","timestamp":"2026-11-06T02:41:20.888Z","isSynthetic":true}
