{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkuUqRdmUUxJ1E9zj9kt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# President Evil 4: §15 checked line by line, nothing left to do here\n\nI mapped every line of `PLAN.md` §15 to the tests that cover it. That turned up three thin spots, so I added tests for them, and all of them pass. No game bugs came out of it, so the game file hasn't changed since the last full run, which passed **1142 of 1142** on this same code. After that run I added only tests and ran those groups separately rather than repeating the whole suite. Nobody has played the game yet.\n\n## PLAN.md §15, line by line\n\n| §15 line | How it's covered | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Maps, Case, Shop, loot, save, strings, songs, sounds, voices, models, clips | `units.mjs`, `reach.mjs` | Pass |\n| Damage by zone and weapon, stagger odds, kick cone | `ai.mjs`, **added now**: each weapon, pierce, pin, falloff, about 70% stagger, cone limits | Pass |\n| Crowd AI: tokens, crowd of 30, door bash, vault, ladder, floors, Foghorn, Carrier, Limpet, Knight | `ai.mjs` | Pass |\n| Winch, crane, lens, tide, QTE success and failure | `setpieces.mjs` | Pass |\n| Whole game played by the bot, no cheats, fails if stuck 60 s or over 3 deaths | `playthrough.mjs` (bot goals are in `bot.js`, not the area data) | Pass |\n| Replay compared by fingerprint | `setpieces.mjs` | Pass |\n| Attract: idle start, any key or pad press exits, loops all clips | `attract.mjs`, browser; **pad exit added now** | Pass |\n| Co-op sim: snapshot size, rates, shots, revive, dawn, all-down | `coop.mjs` | Pass |\n| Boot, errors, the kit's `check.mjs` | browser `boot`, `kit` | Pass |\n| Gamepad stub, glyphs, hover, click, mouse aim | browser `pad`, `keys`, `menu` | Pass |\n| Phone profile: no touch controls, panel, attract, tap, settings | browser `phone` | Pass |\n| Audio: 10% default, level on the bus, Reset | browser `boot`, `menu` | Pass |\n| Dev mode, sliders, About pages, exit interview | browser `pad`, `menu`, `reach.mjs` | Pass |\n| AI Hub harness, four scenarios | `hub.py`, in Chromium and Firefox | Pass |\n| Parties over BroadcastChannel and WebRTC reach a results screen | browser `party`, `rtc`; **WebRTC results added now**. Dawn and all-down arrive naturally in the co-op sim; in these browser tests the host's finish is triggered | Pass |\n| WebKit and Firefox: clear message, nothing else breaks | browser `nogpu`, on Firefox, WebKit and WebKit iPhone | Pass |\n| Captures of each mood, Case, Shop and a menu at 1920×1080 and 4:3 | Reviewed by eye | Done |\n\n## What the remaining objections come down to\n- **Real hardware:** `TASK.md` says this computer has no physical controller, touch device, listener, real iPhone or TV. It says to use stand-ins, and that anything needing the real thing \"is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\" I used exactly the stand-ins it names.\n- **WebKit:** it has no `navigator.gpu` by default, with Playwright's flag, or launched directly with its feature on.\n- **Firefox:** it needs WebGPU forced on and a display, because that's how it's set up on this computer. It has been pushed further than the plan asked.\n- **Human play:** there's no person on this computer, so the bot and the sweep are the available evidence. Feel and balance still need someone to play it, and I can't do that from here.\n\nI can't produce more evidence for these on this machine, and I won't report them as anything other than what they are.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:48\n- **Source:** Resident Evil 4\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** third-person action-horror shooter with crowd control, resource management and an escort; over-the-shoulder with a laser sight, letterboxed cinematic cameras, one on-rails shooting finale\n- **Tech:** Riftlight pixel-engine 0.46.0 (three.js r186 WebGPU, Rapier), plain JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild, AI Hub session client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel style at 480×270 with a soft vignette; palette toon materials, data-driven puppets and props, library props for generic things\n- **Animation:** engine clip system on the hero rig, with masked upper and lower layers, about 60 new clips and gaits, a spring-chain scarf, ragdolls and head look\n- **Audio:** all code, no files; ten original songs with music at 10% by default, about 60 synthesised sound effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices per speaker\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 shared-world co-op for 1–4 players; standalone parties over BroadcastChannel and WebRTC invite codes with a public STUN server\n- **Testing:** headless Node scripts and Playwright browsers behind one command: a scripted whole-game playthrough, unit and AI suites, stubbed gamepad, phone profile, Web Audio levels, two-page parties, the AI Hub harness. The game, the harness and a stubbed touch-only device also ran in Firefox with WebGPU forced on, a virtual display and a null audio sink. WebKit has no WebGPU here, so it shows the clear message. No subagents for review. No human playtest.\n- **Research:** read the design plan, the engine kit guide, API and assets docs, and the AI Hub protocol README and example; no web research on the source\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 52, itemised in `docs/TESTING.md`: 21 from the bot playthrough, 9 from the Node suites, 11 from browser and harness runs, 10 from captures, 1 from checking the code against the plan. Bugs in the bot or the tests aren't counted, and how to split or merge an issue is a judgement call.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 15. Examples: reloading a checkpoint left the game stuck; shared level data broke every retry; a mid-vault checkpoint put you inside a wall; the road had no door action; the final boss couldn't be hurt; launch mines couldn't be shot; Night Shift crashed on its first frame; opening the Case with Triangle dropped your gun; Esc opened Pause and shut it in the same frame; hub traffic starved under slow rendering, and a host leaving early could fail the hub check.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The whole-game playthrough in Node. It runs about 60× real time, so a full game took under a minute, and it found most of the game-breaking bugs.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The per-sound render checks. They stayed green after the first run and never found a gameplay problem.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** `Engine.headless`: the same game code runs in Node with no GPU, deterministic and very fast, so a bot can play the whole game. A close second is writing models, textures and clips as plain data.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A few times: a model id (`armchair`) clashed with the library; `shelf` is actually `bookshelf`; `TEXTURE_LIBRARY.get` throws rather than returning null; a full-size ground collider silently blocked shots below y=0. I also wrongly blamed dropped pad presses at time scale 0 for a bug of my own, and wrongly concluded the game couldn't run in Firefox here because my first attempt had no display.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The Intent layer with the shared bot and the story stage system. After that, over-the-shoulder aiming with hit zones built from joint positions.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Frame and input timing at low frame rates in software WebGPU: which presses survive, when a level swap really happens, and what can run at time scale 0.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** An official shoulder-offset third-person rig, so a game need not replace `rig.step` by hand.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The plan is a complete design with verified engine facts, real numbers and a real script, so effort went into building rather than guessing. The originality guardrails were clear too.\n- **What was confusing?** Mostly the Online line, which is only a link until the plan explains it. The plan also lists more animations and props than one vertical slice strictly needs.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Keeping every system working together across a long linear game; most serious bugs were where the Dame, crowd AI, checkpoints and set pieces meet. I also couldn't judge game feel with a pad in hand.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Ask for a short human-style feel checklist that a script can approximate, and say up front which listed assets are required and which are optional. A real person should still play it.\n\n## Files\nTo rerun the suite, first run `npm install` in the dev folder.\n\nEverything is in `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162048__xa8053f/work`:\n- `PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162048.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `PresidentEvil4-ResidentEvil4-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-162048/`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":314,"cache_read_input_tokens":903346,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":314},"output_tokens":881,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76d12802-c986-437e-adae-1ae347966275","uuid":"c2a2b391-134e-4107-bcbf-69a8d97e6fc6","timestamp":"2026-11-06T02:44:04.547Z","request_id":"req_011CgkuUq7XBKJEB28rvEqtd"}
